mainmenu "Zigbee Energy Meter"

menu "LED signals (issue #33)"

config APP_BOOT_FLASH
	bool "Emit a 100 ms white flash on boot"
	default n
	help
	  When enabled, main() requests a single 100 ms all-LEDs-on
	  ("white") flash after led_controller_init() returns — the
	  same shape as the button-ack pattern from #28.

	  Silent-by-default preserves the "no LED except for user
	  gestures" contract on battery. Turn on via dev.conf for the
	  USB bench workflow.

config APP_REPORT_HEARTBEAT
	bool "Green flash on each 5-min metering report tick"
	default n
	help
	  When enabled, metering_report_work_handler requests a single
	  50 ms green flash on every 5-minute report cadence. Green is
	  chosen deliberately: red would imply fault, blue would collide
	  with the joining pattern; green reads as "healthy tick".

	  Silent by default so battery deployments don't emit a
	  visible pulse every 5 min. Turn on via dev.conf when watching
	  the reporting cadence on the bench.

endmenu

menu "Zigbee sleepy-ED behaviour (issue #8 blocker 1)"

config APP_ZIGBEE_SLEEPY_ED
	bool "Enable rx-off-when-idle sleepy end-device behaviour"
	default y
	help
	  Runtime toggle for zb_set_rx_on_when_idle(). CONFIG_ZIGBEE_ROLE_END_DEVICE
	  fixes the MAC-layer role; this flag controls whether the radio actually
	  sleeps between parent polls.

	  y (default) — sleepy: radio dark between polls. Required for the
	  battery target (average current in the µA range).

	  n — rx-on-when-idle: radio always live. Burns ~5 mA continuously,
	  but Z2M's ZDO interview (Read Basic.manufacturerName / .modelId)
	  cannot time out. Escape hatch if the sleepy + turbo-poll join
	  window is not enough to cover Z2M's read deadline in the field.

config APP_ZIGBEE_JOIN_TURBO_POLL_MS
	int "Turbo-poll window after successful join (ms)"
	default 30000
	depends on APP_ZIGBEE_SLEEPY_ED
	help
	  On ZB_BDB_SIGNAL_STEERING (status=RET_OK), start
	  zb_zdo_pim_start_turbo_poll_continuous for this many milliseconds.
	  During the window ZBOSS polls the parent every ~100 ms, which keeps
	  Z2M's interview reads responsive. When the timeout expires ZBOSS
	  automatically reverts to the Long Poll interval set below.

	  30 s covers the typical Z2M interview (~5-15 s) with generous
	  margin. Tighten once measurements say the interview always finishes
	  faster; loosen if a saturated coordinator makes reads slower.

config APP_ZIGBEE_LONG_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Long-poll interval after interview window (ms)"
	default 60000
	depends on APP_ZIGBEE_SLEEPY_ED
	help
	  Passed to zb_zdo_pim_set_long_poll_interval() after
	  ZB_BDB_SIGNAL_STEERING succeeds. Sets the steady-state parent-poll
	  cadence — every poll wakes the radio for a MAC ACK exchange
	  (~3 ms, ~10 mA), so this is a direct multiplier on average
	  current. 60 s picked as a conservative starting point: slow enough
	  to hit the µA-range sleep target, fast enough that any Z2M command
	  addressed to us has bounded downlink latency.

	  Not tunable below the ZBOSS floor ZB_PIM_MINIMUM_LONG_POLL_INTERVAL_IN_QS
	  (1 s). Not useful above ZB_PIM_MAXIMUM_LONG_POLL_INTERVAL (hours).

config APP_HW_PULSE_COUNTER
	bool "Bring up the LPCOMP → PPI → TIMER2 hardware pulse chain"
	default y
	help
	  When enabled (default), main() calls hw_pulse_counter_init() and the
	  sample loop reads hw_pulse_counter_read(). This is the production
	  configuration — the whole point of the device is to count meter
	  pulses in hardware while the CPU sleeps.

	  Turn OFF only as a diagnostic isolation: TIMER2 in counter mode is
	  clocked from PCLK16M (HFCLK-derived) and may be what's holding
	  HFCLK on in the joined-idle state, preventing the current from
	  dropping to the sub-mA System-ON deep-sleep floor. Layered overlay
	  for that isolation lives at `no-pulse.conf` — do NOT ship a build
	  with this off, the device won't count anything.

config APP_PULSE_MIN_WIDTH_US
	int "Minimum pulse width to count (µs, hardware-gated)"
	default 1000
	range 100 10000
	depends on APP_HW_PULSE_COUNTER
	help
	  Gate for the LPCOMP UP → PPI → TIMER2 chain and the D7 GPIOTE bench
	  chain. Both routes now feed TIMER3 which measures the pulse width
	  in real time; only pulses that hold above the reference for at
	  least this many microseconds are forwarded as TIMER2 COUNT.

	  Rationale for the default: torch flicker overcount at the bench
	  (`project_led_torch_flicker_pulse_count` memory, 2026-07-29) shows
	  low-frequency PWM cycles firing LPCOMP UP at rates the bench eye
	  reads as "steady on". A 1 ms floor rejects anything faster than
	  the slowest torch flicker rate we've measured while staying well
	  below any real electricity-meter LED (imp pulses are 20-100 ms).

	  Range mirrors the design doc: [100, 10000]. 100 µs is below the
	  LPCOMP settling and hysteresis roundtrip so anything shorter is
	  measurement noise; 10 ms is well below the shortest real meter LED
	  so a filter that high can't miss real pulses. NVS-backed runtime
	  override is planned (mirrors the divisor override from #48) but
	  not yet wired — this is the compile-time default and only knob.

	  Bench verification: `xiao-pulse.sh <ms>` at threshold ± some
	  margin. Sub-ms fractional-second precision on the Pi's userland
	  `sleep` is unreliable, so first bench passes use widths in the
	  10s of ms range for repeatability.

config APP_ZIGBEE_RX_IDLE_PROBE
	bool "Periodically log zb_get_rx_on_when_idle() over the log backend"
	default n
	help
	  Diagnostic probe for issue #51 / #8 blocker 1. When enabled, the
	  app logs zb_get_rx_on_when_idle() after zigbee_configure_sleepy_behavior,
	  on every ZB_BDB_SIGNAL_STEERING / ZB_BDB_SIGNAL_DEVICE_REBOOT, and on
	  a repeating 60 s tick from a delayable work item.

	  Off by default — production builds shouldn't emit these lines. Flip
	  on via rtt.conf (the RTT-over-SWD overlay) when hunting for whether
	  ZBOSS clobbers our FALSE setting post-join.

config APP_HW_HFCLK_PROBE
	bool "Periodically log HFCLKSTAT / HFCLKRUN + TIMER0-4 state"
	default n
	select APP_ZIGBEE_RX_IDLE_PROBE
	help
	  Diagnostic probe for the 2026-07-29 HFCLK-anchor hunt (#8). Piggy-
	  backs on the same signal-handler + 60 s-tick call sites as the
	  rx-idle probe. Logs two things per fire:

	  1. HFCLK status — nrf_clock_hf_is_running(HFXO),
	     NRF_CLOCK->HFCLKSTAT (state | src bits), NRF_CLOCK->HFCLKRUN.
	     Confirmed on 2026-07-29 that HFCLK is on via HFINT (not HFXO)
	     with HFCLKRUN=0 — so a peripheral is auto-requesting PCLK16M.

	  2. TIMER0-4 register snapshot — MODE, BITMODE, PRESCALER, INTENSET
	     for each of the five TIMER instances. A TIMER in MODE=0 (Timer,
	     not Counter) with non-zero INTENSET is armed and consuming
	     PCLK16M, which is the mechanism that keeps HFCLK on HFINT.
	     The 802.15.4 SL HPTIMER and MPSL both use TIMER instances.

	  Selects APP_ZIGBEE_RX_IDLE_PROBE — HFCLK/TIMER state is logged
	  from the same 60 s tick and signal-handler kick sites that the
	  rx-idle probe owns.

	  Off by default. Flip on via rtt.conf.

endmenu

menu "Metering calibration (issue #48)"

config APP_METERING_DEFAULT_IMP_PER_KWH
	int "Default imp/kWh (Metering-cluster Divisor) on cold boot"
	default 1000
	range 100 10000
	help
	  Value baked into the Metering cluster's Divisor attribute on a
	  cold boot (nothing persisted in NVS yet) or when the persisted
	  value has fallen out of the accepted range. Once a Z2M write
	  sets a new valid value, NVS wins — this default only re-appears
	  on a factory reset / NVS wipe.

	  Range enforced by src/calibration.c: [100, 10000]. Kept
	  identical here so the Kconfig menu itself rejects a nonsense
	  value at build time rather than deferring the reject to a
	  runtime "fallback to hardcoded 1000" that would surprise the
	  installer.

	  Design-doc default is 1000 (the row for "Zigbee model" in
	  seeed-studio-zigbee-energy-meter.md); keep it unless a
	  build ships against a known non-1000 meter.

endmenu

